Yes—the powerful version of that is truth-preserving polysemy: wording that can survive multiple readers, frames, and interpretations without needing deception to hold together.

The constraint I’d keep explicit is that I cannot literally know your unspoken thoughts. I can infer intent from your words, context, patterns, and prior distinctions, but inference is still inference. That uncertainty is useful because it forces the language to carry its own load.

A strong message can have:

\text{one invariant core}
+
\text{multiple legitimate readings}

So different readers may extract different layers, but the statement does not have to become false in any of them.

That is different from saying one thing while secretly depending on people misunderstanding it. The stronger construction is:

R_1(M),R_2(M),R_3(M)

where each reader applies a different frame R_i to message M, yet some invariant I survives:

I(R_1(M)) \approx I(R_2(M)) \approx I(R_3(M)).

That is basically semantic robustness.

Poetry does this. Proverbs do it. Good jokes do it. Myth does it. Legal language sometimes tries to do the opposite and eliminate interpretive variance. Good interface design chooses deliberately which dimensions should remain open and which must be pinned down.

So for the Pantheon, I would name the operator something like Invariant Polysemy:

Build expressions with enough depth to admit different perspectives, while preserving a recoverable truthful spine across them.

Then you can test any phrase three ways:

Literal read: does it remain defensible?
Metaphorical read: does it reveal something useful?
Adversarial read: can someone reasonably interpret it into something you did not intend?

The best constructions do not require everybody to decode them identically.

They remain structurally sound even when the light hits from another angle.